he test\\n5. PermissionError is subclass of OSError | Status: AVAILABLE | Evidence: Python stdlib \u2014 PermissionError(OSError) is the correct type for EACCES/EPERM scenarios\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED \u2014 DERIVABLE conditions resolvable by agent reading test + implementation files. Design intent determines whether to fix test or fix implementation (prefer fixing implementation to raise PermissionError, since the test was written to test a permission scenario).\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-20T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: Fix wrong exception type in test_atomic_write_cleans_up_temp_file_on_failure\\nGoal: Align the exception type in the test assertion with what the implementation actually raises, strengthening type accuracy.\\n\\nConditions:\\n1. Test file location | Status: DERIVABLE | Info needed: Glob packages/sam_schema/ for test_writers_new.py \u2014 \\\"no tests ran\\\" result suggests file may have moved or pytest config excludes it\\n2. Implementation exception type | Status: AVAILABLE | Info needed: Description states implementation raises OSError\\n3. Test assertion exception type | Status: AVAILABLE | Info needed: Description states test asserts PermissionError\\n4. Design intent: should exception be PermissionError or OSError? | Status: DERIVABLE | Info needed: Read test setup \u2014 if mock injects a permission error scenario, PermissionError is semantically correct and the implementation should be fixed to raise PermissionError; if OSError is intentionally broad, fix the test\\n5. PermissionError is subclass of OSError | Status: AVAILABLE | Evidence: Python stdlib \u2014 PermissionError(OSError) is the correct type for EACCES/EPERM scenarios\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED \u2014 DERIVABLE conditions resolvable by agent reading test + implementation files. Design intent determines whether to fix test or fix implementation (prefer fixing implementation to raise PermissionError, since the test was written to test a permission scenario).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-20T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-20T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **fix wrong exception type in test_atomic_write_cleans_up_temp_file_on_failure** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Agent task \u2014 auto-derived from task description (pre-existing bug, predates deduplicate-agents-phase4)\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-20\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-03-23)\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- [ ] Locate test_writers_new.py in packages/sam_schema/ (file may have moved \u2014 verify path)\\n- [ ] Identify the atomic write implementation that performs cleanup (likely packages/sam_schema/src/ writers module)\\n- [ ] In the cleanup path, re-raise OSError as PermissionError when errno is EACCES or EPERM\\n- [ ] Test test_atomic_write_cleans_up_temp_file_on_failure passes with PermissionError assertion unchanged\\n- [ ] uv run pytest packages/sam_schema/ exits 0\\n- [ ] No new type errors (ty check passes)\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n- `packages/sam_schema/` \u2014 pytest root; locate test_writers_new.py within this tree\\n- Implementation: atomic writer in packages/sam_schema/src/ (exact path DERIVABLE \u2014 search for `temp_file` or `atomic_write` in that tree)\\n- Test: `test_writers_new.py::TestAtomicWriteSafety::test_atomic_write_cleans_up_temp_file_on_failure`\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\nType: defect\\n\\nRoot-Cause Analysis (5-whys):\\n1. Why does the test fail? \u2014 Test asserts PermissionError but implementation raises the less-specific OSError parent.\\n2. Why does the implementation raise OSError? \u2014 The atomic writer likely catches IOError/OSError and re-raises without narrowing to the specific permission sub-type.\\n3. Why was the test written with PermissionError? \u2014 The test scenario simulates a permission-denied failure, so PermissionError is semantically correct \u2014 the test was right; the implementation is imprecise.\\n4. Why was the implementation imprecise? \u2014 Raising the broad OSError is a lazy catch-all; callers lose the ability to distinguish EACCES from other I/O errors.\\n5. Why does this matter beyond the test? \u2014 Any caller using except PermissionError to handle permission failures on atomic writes would silently miss the error.\\n\\nDesign fix: The implementation should raise PermissionError (not OSError) when the underlying OS error is EACCES/EPERM. This is the correct type for the permission-denied scenario the test was designed to cover. Stronger typing, not weaker.\\n\\nFix approach: In the atomic write cleanup path, inspect the OSError and re-raise as PermissionError when errno is EACCES or EPERM. The test assertion stays as PermissionError \u2014 it is correct.\\n\\n## Artifact Manifest\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n<!-- artifact-manifest:begin -->\\n| Type | Path | Status | Agent | Created |\\n|------|------|--------|-------|---------|\\n| task-plan | plan/P779-deduplicate-agents-phase4-followup-1.yaml | current | dh_migrate | 2026-03-24T16:36:01Z |\\n<!-- artifact-manifest:end -->\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"b3060b62e4e6deb409578ad6823d8365922616d8630e99e04b400b65dc4150a7\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCuX1g\"}"}